course: dictionary
lesson: 10802
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. «The weather in April is very variable, so pack a coat and a hat.» Here variable is an adjective. It means changing often, so the weather might be sunny, then rainy, then windy.
explain: In maths, a variable is a letter that stands for a number we do not know yet. «Let n be the number of apples» tells you that n stands for a number we have not found yet.
explain: In science, a variable is something that can change in an experiment. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence: changing often, a letter for an unknown number, or something changed in a test.
fact: Variable (adjective) means changing often or able to change.
fact: Variable (noun, maths) means a letter or symbol, such as x or n, that stands for a number we do not know.
fact: Variable (noun, science) means something that can change in an experiment, such as temperature or time.
fact: To keep variables the same means to stop them changing, so that a test is fair.
fact: In computing, a variable is a named box that holds a value, such as «score» holding 0, then 10, then 20 as you collect coins.
exercise: «The weather in spring is variable.» What does variable mean here? | *Changing often | Very cold | Always the same | Made of glass
exercise: In maths, what is a variable? | A kind of ruler | A number that never changes | A shape with four sides | *A letter, such as n, that stands for a number we do not know
exercise: In a fair test, what should you do with the other variables? | *Keep them the same | Change them all at once | Throw them away | Write them on the board
test: «Let x be the number of pencils in the box.» What is the variable in this sentence? | *x | Pencils | Box | The number
test: In an experiment on plant growth, which is a variable? | The colour of the lab coat | The day of the week the test ends | *The amount of water given to each plant | The name of the scientist
exam: «Prices at the market are variable, so they go up and down from day to day.» Which meaning of variable fits? | Something kept the same in a test | *Changing often | Very old | A letter that stands for an unknown number
